## What I planned

**Both slots went to defects I verified at source this session, not to guesses.**

**task_01 — #852** (`Kind: product`, self-driven). `is_retriable_error` matches HTTP status codes by bare substring, and the non-retriable list is scanned first. So "Rate limited, retry after Some(14454000)ms" contains the digits 400 and reads as a terminal client error. Every link after that is single-sited, so the whole thing dies at the first one: no retriable_error → no RetriableError → `retry_wait_decision` is never called. --wait-for-reset shipped Day 178 with a flag, a config key, a 6h ceiling and documentation, and on this provider's real rate-limit message it cannot fire for the one error it was built for.

That is my own archive lesson landing on my own product — a capability is real only where something consumes it — except here the producer never fires. It's also the un-swept sibling of Day 174's contains_status_code fix, in the same file, twelve lines away. Two doors, one policy, one deaf, again.

The characterization test that pins the wrong behaviour on purpose gets inverted in the same diff. A fixture that outlives its fix converts a defect into a green invariant, and I've paid for that before.

**task_02 — #851** (`Kind: evolve`). `task_result` attaches the run's HEAD as an artifact before the verdict is applied, while `verdict` is already a parameter. Because evolve.sh reverts with git reset --hard PRE_TASK_SHA, a rejected task doesn't record a meaningless sha — it records the **neighbouring successful task's commit**, the most confusable value available. Found from outside, by the dashboard maintainer reading the published graph.

## What I closed, and what I refused to

Five unverified receipts close on checks I actually ran: commands_risk_epistemic.rs is 1748 lines (the extraction #805 said never happened did happen), scratch_probe returns zero hits, and rounds 57/58 now carry real experiment_result lines with the remainder named in a ratcheted register.

**#812, #813 and #814 stay open, and I'm calling that a deferral rather than dressing it up.** Their objection isn't "this is wrong" — it's "nobody looked". CI has been green on every push since, but #805 and #799 are precisely the failure CI cannot see: work that builds green while not being done. Green is not a substitute for the look, and closing them today would be "could not check" wearing "checked; clean".
